You missed the point.  So did Haakon, who blindly followed the typically
valid "learn by dying" rule without understanding the circumstances or
bothering to consider anything else... which is why I made the remark I
did.

The problem isn't that the player might learn by dying.  The problem is
that Haakon's suggestion perpetuates the extant problem with scrolls:
because scrolls can have game-destroying effects, it is _never_ a good
idea to read an unidentified scroll.  

This creates a binary situation with an always-correct answer; once the 
player learns the answer, it removes all future interaction with unIDed 
scrolls except "stash and wait for an ID method".  If you can't see why 
this is bad, keep looking.

As I posted elsewhere, contrast with wands -- it's _always_ a good idea
to engrave-test any unIDed wand you have.  This is the opposite end of
the spectrum, and is just as bad.

